You can end a C program a few common ways—like choosing whether to take the cake out of the oven normally or shut everything down immediately:

```c
return 0;
```

Place this at the end of `main()` for a successful, normal exit:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    printf("Freshly baked!\n");
    return 0;
}
```

You can also use `exit()` from `<stdlib.h>`:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
}
```

For an error exit:

```c
exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
```

Or return a nonzero status from `main`:

```c
return 1;
```

A few useful distinctions:

- `return` from `main` ends the program normally.
- `exit()` ends the program from anywhere and runs registered cleanup handlers (`atexit`) and flushes standard I/O.
- `_Exit()` / `quick_exit()` terminate with less cleanup, useful only in special cases.
- `abort()` immediately terminates abnormally, often producing a crash report/core dump.

For most recipes, `return 0;` at the end of `main()` is the perfect golden-brown finish.
